/* Bassiet book, Depth 13: The Sleeping Cloisters (reproduction). Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c13-cycle']={depth:13,title:'The Turning Seasons',
epi:'In these cloisters the hormones rise and fall like seasons, on a calendar nothing else in the body keeps.',
c:['menstrual-cycle','pcos','sex-hormones'],
S:[
{h:'The menstrual cycle',fig:'cycle',p:[
'**Follicular phase** (variable length, and the reason cycles differ between women): FSH rescues a cohort of follicles from atresia. One becomes dominant because it carries the most FSH receptors and aromatase, and its oestradiol and **inhibin B** then starve its rivals of FSH. In the **two-cell model**, theca cells make androgens under LH and granulosa cells convert them to **oestradiol** with aromatase under FSH. Oestradiol rebuilds the functional layer of the endometrium (proliferative phase) and makes cervical mucus thin and stretchy. Once oestradiol has stayed high for about two days, its feedback on the pituitary flips from negative to **positive**, and the **LH surge** follows.',
'**Ovulation** comes about 36 hours after the surge begins (mittelschmerz, clear stretchy mucus). LH also luteinises the granulosa cells, which start making progesterone. **Luteal phase** (about 14 days, fixed): the corpus luteum secretes **progesterone** and oestradiol, turning the endometrium **secretory** (coiled glycogen-rich glands, spiral arteries, oedematous stroma), thickening cervical mucus and raising basal body temperature by about 0.3–0.5 °C. Without hCG from an implanting embryo the corpus luteum regresses to a corpus albicans; progesterone falls, the spiral arteries constrict and the functional layer is shed as **menstruation**. Because the luteal phase is fixed, ovulation happens about 14 days before the next period, not 14 days after the last one.'],
table:{cols:['Phase','Main hormone','Ovary','Endometrium'],rows:[
['Menstrual (days 1–5)','all low; FSH begins to rise','new cohort recruited','functional layer shed'],
['Follicular','oestradiol ↑','one dominant follicle','proliferative: straight tubular glands'],
['Ovulation','LH (and FSH) surge','follicle ruptures','late proliferative'],
['Luteal','progesterone ↑ (and oestradiol)','corpus luteum','secretory: coiled glands, glycogen, spiral arteries']]},
s:[['pharm','Combined pill, patch and ring: oestrogen and progestin feedback suppress FSH and the LH surge, so there is no ovulation; the progestin also thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium. Avoid in smokers over 35, migraine with aura, past venous thrombosis or thrombophilia, breast cancer, active liver disease, uncontrolled hypertension and the first weeks after delivery. They lower ovarian and endometrial cancer risk. Progestin-only methods (pill, implant, depot injection, levonorgestrel intrauterine system) suit women who cannot take oestrogen; the depot injection thins bone. Emergency contraception: copper IUD (up to 5 days, the most effective, no hormones), ulipristal (progesterone receptor modulator, up to 5 days), levonorgestrel (best within 3 days).'],['phys','Inhibin B from granulosa cells feeds back on FSH alone; activin raises it. Progesterone is thermogenic, which is why a basal temperature chart only shows that ovulation has already happened.']]},
{h:'Puberty and menopause',p:[
'Puberty begins when the hypothalamus starts releasing **pulsatile GnRH** (continuous GnRH does the opposite and shuts the pituitary down). Girls: breast buds (thelarche), pubic and axillary hair (driven by adrenal androgens), the growth spurt, then menarche about 2 years after thelarche. Boys: testicular enlargement first, then pubic hair, penile growth and a later, larger growth spurt. Oestrogen, in both sexes, finally fuses the growth plates. **Precocious puberty**: central (early activation of the GnRH pulse generator; idiopathic in most girls, hypothalamic hamartoma or other lesions; LH and FSH raised; treat with a continuous GnRH agonist) or peripheral (GnRH-independent: CAH, sex steroid–secreting tumours, McCune-Albright syndrome, exogenous hormones; LH and FSH suppressed). **Kallmann syndrome**: GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode alongside the olfactory axons, giving anosmia, delayed puberty and low LH and FSH; pulsatile GnRH or gonadotropins restore fertility.',
'**Menopause** (average age 51; diagnosed after 12 months without a period): the follicles run out, oestrogen and inhibin fall, and **FSH rises** (the best marker), with LH following. Oestrone made by aromatase in fat becomes the main oestrogen. Hot flushes, night sweats, vaginal atrophy, sleep disturbance, mood change, accelerated bone loss and rising cardiovascular risk follow. Menopause before 40 is **premature ovarian insufficiency** (Turner syndrome, fragile X premutation, autoimmune oophoritis, chemotherapy or pelvic radiotherapy, surgery).'],
s:[['pharm','Hormone therapy is the most effective treatment for hot flushes. A woman with a uterus needs a progestin with the oestrogen, because oestrogen alone causes endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma. Risks rise with age and duration: venous thrombosis and stroke (less with transdermal oestrogen) and breast cancer with combined therapy. Contraindications: unexplained vaginal bleeding, oestrogen-dependent cancer, active thrombosis, liver disease. Non-hormonal options: SSRIs or SNRIs, gabapentin, and fezolinetant (neurokinin-3 receptor antagonist acting on the hypothalamic thermostat). Raloxifene (a SERM) protects bone and lowers breast cancer risk without stimulating the endometrium, but raises clot risk and can worsen flushes.']]},
{h:'When cycles fail',p:[
'**Primary amenorrhoea** (no periods by 15 with normal growth and secondary sexual characteristics, or by 13 with no breast development): Turner syndrome (streak ovaries, high FSH), Müllerian agenesis (normal breasts and pubic hair, 46,XX, absent uterus and upper vagina), complete androgen insensitivity (46,XY, breasts from aromatised testosterone, scant pubic hair, blind vagina, testes inside), imperforate hymen or transverse septum (cyclic pain, haematocolpos), Kallmann syndrome, constitutional delay and CAH.',
'**Secondary amenorrhoea** (periods stop for 3 months or more): first exclude **pregnancy**, then measure prolactin, TSH and FSH. **Hyperprolactinaemia** (prolactinoma, dopamine-blocking drugs such as antipsychotics and metoclopramide, hypothyroidism because TRH stimulates prolactin) suppresses GnRH, causing amenorrhoea and galactorrhoea. **Functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea** follows low weight, heavy exercise or stress. Other causes: PCOS, premature ovarian insufficiency, **Sheehan syndrome** (pituitary infarction after postpartum haemorrhage: failed lactation first), and **Asherman syndrome** (intrauterine adhesions after curettage, with normal hormones).',
'**Polycystic ovary syndrome** is diagnosed with two of three features, once mimics are excluded: oligo- or anovulation, clinical or biochemical androgen excess, and polycystic ovaries on ultrasound. Insulin resistance sits at its centre: insulin acts with LH on theca cells to raise androgen output and lowers sex hormone–binding globulin, so free testosterone climbs; fat aromatises androgens to oestrone, and with no ovulation there is no progesterone to oppose it. Consequences: hirsutism and acne, infertility, endometrial hyperplasia and **endometrial carcinoma**, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, sleep apnoea. Treatment: weight loss; the combined pill (regular bleeds, more SHBG, endometrial protection); metformin; spironolactone for hirsutism; letrozole (first line) or clomiphene to induce ovulation.'],
table:{cols:['Pattern','FSH / LH','Oestradiol','Causes'],rows:[
['Hypothalamic or pituitary failure','low or normal','low','low weight, exercise, stress, Kallmann, hyperprolactinaemia, Sheehan'],
['Ovarian failure','high','low','Turner, premature ovarian insufficiency, chemotherapy, radiotherapy'],
['PCOS','LH high relative to FSH','normal','androgen excess, anovulation'],
['Outflow tract','normal','normal','Müllerian agenesis, imperforate hymen, septum, Asherman']]},
key:'The luteal phase is fixed at 14 days. In amenorrhoea, rule out pregnancy first, then read FSH: high means the ovary has failed; low means the brain has stopped asking.',
s:[['path','Endometriosis: endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus (ovary "chocolate cysts", pouch of Douglas, uterosacral ligaments), probably seeded by retrograde menstruation: cyclic pelvic pain, painful intercourse and defecation, infertility, with a normal-sized uterus. Adenomyosis: glands within the myometrium; an enlarged, soft, boggy uterus with heavy painful periods. Leiomyomas (fibroids): the commonest uterine tumour, benign smooth muscle, oestrogen-dependent, whorled on section, may grow in pregnancy and shrink after menopause; they do not turn into leiomyosarcoma, which arises on its own in older women.'],['pharm','Clomiphene blocks oestrogen receptors in the hypothalamus, so the brain senses too little oestrogen and raises GnRH, FSH and LH; risks are hot flushes, visual disturbance, multiple pregnancy and ovarian hyperstimulation. Letrozole (aromatase inhibitor) lowers oestrogen briefly to the same end, with fewer multiple pregnancies. Endometriosis pain: NSAIDs, the combined pill or a progestin, GnRH agonists or antagonists (elagolix), and surgery.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'What triggers the LH surge?',o:['Falling progesterone levels','A rise in FSH alone','Sustained high oestradiol','Rising inhibin B output'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'menstrual-cycle',x:'After about two days of high oestradiol, its feedback on the pituitary turns positive and LH surges; ovulation follows about 36 hours later. Falling progesterone triggers menstruation, not the surge.'},
{q:'Best single hormone marker of menopause?',o:['FSH','LH','Oestradiol','Progesterone'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'menstrual-cycle',x:'With no follicles left, inhibin and oestradiol fall and FSH climbs, so FSH is the most sensitive marker. LH also rises, but later and less; oestradiol fluctuates widely in the perimenopause and is unreliable.'},
{q:'Hirsutism, irregular periods and obesity with a high LH:FSH ratio. Which cancer risk is raised?',o:['Epithelial ovarian','Squamous cervical','Lobular breast','Endometrial'],a:3,d:'path',c:'pcos',x:'Without ovulation there is no corpus luteum and no progesterone, so oestrogen acts on the endometrium unopposed and hyperplasia can progress to endometrioid carcinoma. Ovarian cancer is not clearly raised in PCOS, since fewer ovulations, if anything, protect the ovary.'},
{q:'A 16-year-old has no periods, no sense of smell and low LH and FSH. Diagnosis?',o:['Turner syndrome','Müllerian agenesis','Kallmann syndrome','Androgen insensitivity'],a:2,d:'path',c:'sex-hormones',x:'GnRH neurons migrate with the olfactory axons, so when migration fails the patient has anosmia plus low GnRH, LH and FSH. Turner syndrome also causes primary amenorrhoea, but with high FSH, because the ovaries themselves have failed.'},
{q:'Which is the most effective emergency contraception up to 5 days after intercourse?',o:['Levonorgestrel pill','Copper IUD','Combined pill','Progestin implant'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'menstrual-cycle',x:'A copper IUD is toxic to sperm and prevents implantation, works up to 5 days after intercourse and fails in under 1%. Levonorgestrel only delays ovulation, so it fails if the LH surge has already begun and works best within 3 days.'},
{q:'Clomiphene is given to induce ovulation in PCOS. How does it work?',o:['Hypothalamic oestrogen receptor block','Inhibition of ovarian aromatase enzyme','Agonism at pituitary GnRH receptors','Blocks endometrial progesterone receptors'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'pcos',x:'Clomiphene is a SERM that blocks oestrogen receptors in the hypothalamus, so negative feedback is lost and GnRH, FSH and LH rise. Aromatase inhibition is how letrozole works, and letrozole is now the preferred first-line drug in PCOS.'},
{q:'Periods stop after curettage for retained products. FSH, prolactin and TSH are normal, and an oestrogen-progestin course brings no bleed. Cause?',o:['Sheehan syndrome','Asherman syndrome','Early menopause','Prolactinoma'],a:1,d:'path',c:'menstrual-cycle',x:'Curettage can strip the basal endometrium and leave adhesions, so the hormones arrive but there is no lining to grow and shed. Sheehan syndrome follows postpartum haemorrhage, with low pituitary hormones and failed lactation.'}
]};

BOOK['c13-preg']={depth:13,title:'The Second Heartbeat',
epi:'Plans for a second heartbeat, written in hormone and blood. The lamp reads what a pregnancy demands of a body, and the quiet ways it fails.',
c:['pregnancy','preeclampsia','trophoblastic','water-balance','coagulation'],
S:[
{h:'Hormones of pregnancy',p:[
'After implantation (about day 6 after fertilisation) the **syncytiotrophoblast** makes **hCG**, which keeps the corpus luteum alive and making progesterone until the placenta takes over at about 8–10 weeks (the luteo-placental shift). hCG doubles about every 48 hours early on, peaks at about 10 weeks and is what pregnancy tests detect. hCG shares its α subunit with TSH, LH and FSH, so very high hCG (twins, molar pregnancy) can cause hyperthyroidism and hyperemesis.',
'**Progesterone** keeps the myometrium quiet and relaxes smooth muscle everywhere: reflux, constipation, dilated ureters (pyelonephritis risk) and a higher ventilation drive. **Human placental lactogen**, with placental growth hormone, progesterone and cortisol, causes insulin resistance in the second half of pregnancy; this shunts glucose to the fetus and unmasks gestational diabetes. **Oestriol** needs the fetus to make it: fetal adrenal DHEA-S is hydroxylated in the fetal liver and aromatised by the placenta, so a low oestriol hints at fetal or placental trouble. Prolactin rises throughout, but high oestrogen and progesterone block milk production until delivery.'],
table:{cols:['Change','Direction'],rows:[
['Cardiac output','↑ 30–50% (stroke volume first, then heart rate)'],
['Systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure','↓, lowest in the second trimester'],
['Plasma volume','↑ more than red cell mass: dilutional anaemia'],
['Renin, aldosterone','↑ (sodium and water retention)'],
['GFR and renal plasma flow','↑ about 50%: creatinine and urea fall; glycosuria'],
['Tidal volume, minute ventilation','↑ (progesterone): PaCO₂ about 30 mmHg, mild compensated respiratory alkalosis'],
['Functional residual capacity','↓ (diaphragm pushed up)'],
['Clotting factors','↑ (VII, VIII, X, fibrinogen): thrombosis risk up to 6 weeks after delivery'],
['White cells, ESR, alkaline phosphatase','↑ (ALP from the placenta)'],
['Thyroxine-binding globulin','↑: total T4 up, free T4 normal'],
['Gastric emptying, gut motility','slower: reflux, constipation']]},
s:[['phys','Fetal haemoglobin (α₂γ₂) binds 2,3-BPG poorly, so it holds oxygen more tightly than adult haemoglobin and pulls oxygen across the placenta. Maternal hyperventilation lowers maternal PaCO₂, which helps fetal CO₂ diffuse the other way.']]},
{h:'Early loss and the molar growths',p:[
'**Ectopic pregnancy** (most in the ampulla of the fallopian tube; risk with previous PID, previous ectopic, tubal surgery, IVF, pregnancy with an IUD in place): amenorrhoea, pain and bleeding; hCG rising too slowly with an empty uterus on ultrasound; rupture causes haemoperitoneum and shock. Methotrexate if stable, small and unruptured; surgery otherwise. **Miscarriage** in the first trimester is usually chromosomal (autosomal trisomies most often): threatened (bleeding, closed os, viable), inevitable (os open), incomplete (tissue retained), complete, or missed (no heartbeat, closed os). Recurrent loss: antiphospholipid syndrome, uterine anomalies, parental balanced translocations.',
'**Gestational trophoblastic disease** grows from placental tissue and is tracked by hCG. **Choriocarcinoma** is malignant trophoblast without villi; it can follow a mole, a miscarriage or a normal pregnancy, makes very high hCG, spreads early through the blood to the lungs ("cannonball" shadows) and vagina, and is very sensitive to methotrexate. After a mole is evacuated, hCG is followed down to zero; a plateau or rise means persistent disease.'],
table:{cols:['Feature','Complete mole','Partial mole'],rows:[
['Karyotype','46,XX (sometimes 46,XY), all paternal: empty egg','69,XXY or 69,XXX (two sperm, one egg)'],
['Fetal parts','none','present'],
['hCG','very high','raised, less so'],
['Uterus','often larger than dates','often normal or small for dates'],
['Ultrasound','"snowstorm", no fetus','fetal parts, cystic placenta'],
['p57 staining','negative (no maternal genes)','positive'],
['Complications','hyperemesis, hyperthyroidism, early pre-eclampsia, theca lutein cysts','fewer'],
['Persistent disease','15–20% (choriocarcinoma about 2–3%)','under 5%; choriocarcinoma rare']]},
s:[['micro','TORCH infections cross the placenta. Toxoplasma (cat faeces, undercooked meat): hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, diffuse intracranial calcification. Others: syphilis (stillbirth; later notched teeth, saddle nose, sabre shins, rhinitis, rash), parvovirus B19 (fetal anaemia, hydrops), varicella (limb hypoplasia, skin scarring), Zika (microcephaly), Listeria (unpasteurised cheese; amnionitis, preterm birth, neonatal sepsis; ampicillin). Rubella: cataracts, deafness, PDA, "blueberry muffin" rash (vaccinate before pregnancy, never during). CMV: the commonest; deafness, periventricular calcification. HSV: vesicles, encephalitis; caesarean if lesions are active at labour. Group B streptococcus colonises the vagina and rectum; in the US women are screened at 36–37 weeks and carriers get intrapartum penicillin to prevent early-onset neonatal sepsis and meningitis.']]},
{h:'Late bleeding',p:[
'Bleeding after 20 weeks: **placenta praevia** (placenta over or near the internal os; painless bright red bleeding, soft non-tender uterus; never do a digital vaginal examination until ultrasound has placed the placenta), **placental abruption** (the placenta separates early; painful bleeding, a hard tender uterus, fetal distress, sometimes concealed; risks are hypertension, cocaine, smoking, trauma; can trigger DIC) and **vasa praevia** (fetal vessels run across the internal os; painless bleeding as the membranes rupture, with sudden fetal bradycardia because the blood lost is fetal; emergency caesarean). **Uterine rupture** (previous caesarean scar): sudden pain, loss of contractions and fetal station, fetal distress.',
'**Placenta accreta spectrum**: accreta (villi attach to the myometrium because the decidua basalis is deficient), increta (villi invade into the myometrium), percreta (through the serosa, sometimes into the bladder). The main risk is a previous caesarean combined with a placenta praevia lying over the scar. The placenta will not separate at delivery, causing massive haemorrhage that often needs hysterectomy.',
'**Postpartum haemorrhage** (more than 500 mL vaginally or 1000 mL at caesarean): think of tone, trauma, tissue and thrombin. Uterine atony is the commonest cause: bimanual massage and oxytocin, then carboprost (avoid in asthma), methylergonovine (avoid in hypertension), misoprostol, and tranexamic acid early; then balloon tamponade or surgery. Severe haemorrhage can infarct the enlarged pituitary of pregnancy (**Sheehan syndrome**): failure to lactate, then amenorrhoea, hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency.'],
key:'Painless and soft: praevia. Painful and hard: abruption. Bleeding at membrane rupture with fetal bradycardia: vasa praevia. A placenta that will not let go after a caesarean: accreta spectrum.'},
{h:'Hypertension and diabetes in pregnancy',p:[
'**Gestational hypertension** is new hypertension after 20 weeks without proteinuria. **Pre-eclampsia** adds proteinuria or organ damage. Its root is a shallow placenta: trophoblast fails to remodel the maternal spiral arteries into wide low-resistance channels, the ischaemic placenta releases antiangiogenic factors (soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 traps VEGF and PlGF), and the maternal endothelium fails everywhere. Risks: first pregnancy, previous pre-eclampsia, chronic hypertension, diabetes, kidney disease, antiphospholipid syndrome, multiple pregnancy, molar pregnancy (which can give it before 20 weeks). Severe features: BP 160/110 or more, headache, visual disturbance, epigastric or right upper quadrant pain, pulmonary oedema, low platelets, rising creatinine and transaminases. **Eclampsia** adds seizures; **HELLP** is haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets.',
'Treatment: **magnesium sulfate** for seizure prophylaxis and treatment (watch for lost tendon reflexes, then respiratory depression; calcium gluconate reverses it), antihypertensives (labetalol, nifedipine, hydralazine) and **delivery**, the only cure. Low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks lowers the risk in high-risk women.',
'**Gestational diabetes** follows the insulin resistance of late pregnancy: screen at 24–28 weeks with a glucose tolerance test. Maternal glucose crosses the placenta but insulin does not, so the fetus makes its own excess insulin: macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, polyhydramnios, and neonatal hypoglycaemia once the maternal glucose supply is cut. Diet first, then insulin or metformin. Pre-existing diabetes with poor control at conception adds congenital heart disease (transposition, VSD), caudal regression and neural tube defects, so control should be tight before conception.'],
s:[['pharm','Generally safe in pregnancy: labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa, hydralazine; insulin and metformin; heparins (do not cross the placenta); penicillins, cephalosporins, azithromycin, nitrofurantoin (not at term); paracetamol; levothyroxine (dose often rises). Folic acid 0.4 mg daily from before conception; a high dose (4–5 mg) after a previous neural tube defect, and often for women on valproate or carbamazepine.']]},
{h:'What crosses the placenta',p:[
'The embryo is most vulnerable during organogenesis, about weeks 3 to 8 after fertilisation. Before that an exposure is usually all or nothing (loss, or full recovery); later exposures affect growth and the function of organs already formed (kidneys, brain, teeth).'],
table:{cols:['Agent','Fetal effect'],rows:[
['ACE inhibitors, ARBs','renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia (mainly second and third trimester)'],
['Warfarin','nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses (weeks 6–12); fetal brain bleeding later'],
['Valproate','neural tube defects, lower IQ and autism risk; the most teratogenic antiepileptic'],
['Carbamazepine','neural tube defects, craniofacial and fingernail hypoplasia'],
['Phenytoin','fetal hydantoin syndrome: cleft lip and palate, hypoplastic nails and distal phalanges, microcephaly'],
['Lithium','Ebstein anomaly (tricuspid valve displaced toward the apex)'],
['Isotretinoin, acitretin','craniofacial, CNS and heart defects, miscarriage'],
['Methotrexate','miscarriage; skull and limb defects'],
['Thalidomide','limb reduction (phocomelia)'],
['Tetracyclines','discoloured teeth, impaired bone growth'],
['Aminoglycosides','eighth nerve damage (deafness)'],
['Diethylstilboestrol','clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina and uterine anomalies in daughters'],
['Alcohol','fetal alcohol syndrome: smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, short palpebral fissures, microcephaly, intellectual disability; the commonest preventable cause'],
['Smoking, cocaine','growth restriction, preterm birth; cocaine also abruption'],
['Iodine lack or excess','congenital goitre and hypothyroidism']]},
key:'Ebstein from lithium, phocomelia from thalidomide, spina bifida from valproate, nasal hypoplasia from warfarin, dead kidneys from ACE inhibitors.'},
{h:'Amniotic fluid, screening and the newborn',p:[
'After about 16 weeks most amniotic fluid is fetal urine, and the fetus swallows it back. **Oligohydramnios** (too little): renal agenesis or obstruction (posterior urethral valves), placental insufficiency, ruptured membranes; prolonged, it causes the **Potter sequence** (flat face, limb deformities such as clubfeet, pulmonary hypoplasia). **Polyhydramnios** (too much): failure to swallow (oesophageal or duodenal atresia, anencephaly), maternal diabetes (fetal polyuria), twins, fetal anaemia.',
'**Screening for aneuploidy**: first trimester, low PAPP-A, high hCG and increased nuchal translucency in Down syndrome. Second-trimester quad screen: Down syndrome gives low AFP, high hCG, low oestriol and high inhibin A; Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18) lowers AFP, hCG and oestriol together, with inhibin normal. Cell-free fetal DNA in maternal blood is the most sensitive screen; chorionic villus sampling or amniocentesis confirms. **Neural tube defects** and abdominal wall defects raise maternal AFP (as do twins and wrong dates).',
'**Rh disease**: an Rh(D)-negative mother exposed to Rh-positive fetal blood (at delivery, miscarriage, trauma) makes IgG anti-D, which crosses the placenta in the next Rh-positive pregnancy and causes haemolysis, fetal anaemia and hydrops. Anti-D immunoglobulin at about 28 weeks and within 72 hours of delivery or any sensitising event prevents it. ABO incompatibility (group O mother, A or B baby) is commoner, milder, and can affect a first pregnancy.'],
s:[['phys','At birth the first breaths expand the lungs, pulmonary vascular resistance falls, left atrial pressure rises and pushes the flap of the foramen ovale shut; rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins close the ductus arteriosus (indomethacin closes a patent one; prostaglandin E1 keeps it open in duct-dependent heart disease). Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome comes from too little surfactant: prematurity, maternal diabetes (fetal insulin delays surfactant) and caesarean without labour; a lecithin:sphingomyelin ratio under 2 in amniotic fluid predicts it, and betamethasone given to the mother before a preterm delivery prevents much of it.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Painless bright red bleeding at 33 weeks with a soft, non-tender uterus. What must not be done before imaging?',o:['Transabdominal ultrasound','Fetal monitoring','Digital vaginal examination','Full blood count'],a:2,d:'path',c:'pregnancy',x:'Painless third-trimester bleeding suggests placenta praevia, and a finger through the cervix can tear the low-lying placenta and cause torrential bleeding, so ultrasound must locate the placenta first. Abruption, the main alternative, is painful with a hard, tender uterus.'},
{q:'Seizures in a woman at 36 weeks with hypertension and proteinuria. First drug?',o:['Magnesium sulfate','Phenytoin','IV diazepam','Labetalol'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'preeclampsia',x:'Magnesium sulfate both prevents and treats eclamptic seizures, and works better than phenytoin or diazepam; delivery is the definitive cure. Labetalol controls blood pressure but does not stop seizures.'},
{q:'Uterus larger than dates, very high hCG, a snowstorm pattern and no fetal parts. Karyotype?',o:['69,XXY (two sperm, one egg)','45,X (monosomy X)','47,XXY (non-disjunction)','46,XX, all paternal'],a:3,d:'path',c:'trophoblastic',x:'A complete mole forms when an egg with no nucleus is fertilised by a sperm that duplicates (or by two sperm), giving 46,XX with only paternal DNA and no fetus. 69,XXY is the triploid partial mole, which has fetal parts and lower hCG.'},
{q:'Which pregnancy change best explains a serum creatinine of 40 µmol/L (0.45 mg/dL)?',o:['Low muscle mass','Low protein intake','Raised GFR','Dilution only'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'pregnancy',x:'Renal plasma flow and GFR rise by about half early in pregnancy, so creatinine and urea fall; a creatinine that would be normal outside pregnancy can mean renal impairment in it. Plasma volume rises too, but creatinine falls mainly because clearance rises.'},
{q:'Oligohydramnios, a flat face, clubfeet and pulmonary hypoplasia. Cause?',o:['Duodenal atresia','Anencephaly','Poorly controlled maternal diabetes','Bilateral renal agenesis'],a:3,d:'path',c:'pregnancy',x:'Fetal urine makes most amniotic fluid after 16 weeks, so absent kidneys leave too little fluid; the uterus compresses the fetus and the lungs cannot grow. The other three options cause polyhydramnios, through failed swallowing or, in diabetes, fetal polyuria.'},
{q:'Quad screen: AFP low, hCG high, oestriol low, inhibin A high. Most likely fetal condition?',o:['Trisomy 18','Trisomy 21','Neural tube defect','Twin pregnancy'],a:1,d:'path',c:'aneuploidy',x:'Down syndrome lowers AFP and oestriol and raises hCG and inhibin A. Trisomy 18 lowers AFP, hCG and oestriol together, while a neural tube defect or twins raise AFP.'},
{q:'After two caesareans, a woman has a placenta praevia over the scar. At delivery the placenta will not separate and she bleeds heavily. Underlying defect?',o:['Deficient decidua basalis','Villi invading through the serosa','Uterine atony alone','A retained extra lobe'],a:0,d:'path',c:'pregnancy',x:'In placenta accreta the decidua basalis is missing, often over a caesarean scar, so villi attach directly to the myometrium and cannot separate. Villi through the serosa define percreta, the most severe end of the spectrum, and atony is a consequence rather than the cause here.'}
]};

BOOK['c13-gonad']={depth:13,title:'The Seed Halls',
epi:'Halls of sleeping seed cells, waiting to make the next generation. Now and then one wakes wrong and will not stop dividing.',
c:['testis','ovarian-tumours','prostate','cervix-uterus','sex-hormones','sti','p53-rb'],
S:[
{h:'The testis',p:[
'**Leydig cells** make testosterone under **LH**. **Sertoli cells** (under FSH and local testosterone) nurse developing sperm, form the blood-testis barrier with tight junctions, and make **inhibin B** (feedback to FSH), androgen-binding protein and **anti-Müllerian hormone** (regresses the Müllerian ducts in the male fetus; how the ducts and genitalia first diverge is read in the First Folds). Testosterone feeds back on LH; inhibin feeds back on FSH. Testosterone is converted to **DHT** by 5α-reductase (external genitalia, prostate, hair loss) and to oestradiol by aromatase. Spermatogenesis takes about 74 days and needs a temperature a few degrees below the core.',
'Exogenous testosterone or anabolic steroids suppress LH and FSH, so the testes shrink and sperm production stops while the body looks well androgenised. **Klinefelter syndrome** (47,XXY): tall, small firm testes, gynaecomastia, infertility; Sertoli and Leydig damage gives low inhibin and testosterone with high FSH and LH. Lymph from the testis follows the testicular vessels to the **para-aortic nodes**; the scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. The left testicular vein empties into the left renal vein, the right straight into the inferior vena cava.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Use and cautions'],rows:[
['Finasteride, dutasteride','5α-reductase inhibitors','BPH, male-pattern baldness; lower PSA by about half'],
['Tamsulosin','α₁A blocker (prostate)','BPH symptoms; postural hypotension, floppy iris in cataract surgery'],
['Sildenafil, tadalafil','PDE-5 inhibitors (more cGMP)','erectile dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension; never with nitrates; blue vision (sildenafil)'],
['Leuprolide (continuous), goserelin','GnRH agonists: brief stimulation, then pituitary down-regulation','prostate cancer (initial flare: cover with an androgen blocker), endometriosis, central precocious puberty'],
['Degarelix','GnRH antagonist','prostate cancer; suppresses at once, no flare'],
['Flutamide, bicalutamide, enzalutamide','androgen receptor blockers','prostate cancer; gynaecomastia, liver toxicity (flutamide)'],
['Abiraterone','CYP17 (17α-hydroxylase) inhibitor','castration-resistant prostate cancer; give with prednisone (mineralocorticoid excess)'],
['Spironolactone, ketoconazole','anti-androgens (receptor block; steroid synthesis block)','hirsutism; gynaecomastia as a side effect']]},
s:[['path','Cryptorchidism (undescended testis): infertility and germ cell tumour risk (in both testes); orchidopexy before 1 year. Testicular torsion: sudden severe pain, high-riding horizontal testis, absent cremasteric reflex; untwist surgically within 6 hours and fix both sides. Varicocele: dilated pampiniform plexus, usually left ("bag of worms", larger on standing), reduces fertility; a sudden right or non-collapsing varicocele raises suspicion of a renal or retroperitoneal mass. Hydrocele: fluid in a patent or reactivated tunica vaginalis; transilluminates.']]},
{h:'Testicular and prostate tumours',p:[
'**Germ cell tumours** make up about 95% of testicular tumours: young men, a painless mass that does not transilluminate. Risk: cryptorchidism, Klinefelter, family history; most arise from germ cell neoplasia in situ and carry an isochromosome of 12p. Never biopsy through the scrotum, which would open a new lymphatic route to the inguinal nodes; remove the testis through the groin. Non-germ cell tumours: **Leydig cell tumour** (Reinke crystals; androgens cause precocious puberty in boys, oestrogens cause gynaecomastia in men) and **lymphoma** (the commonest testicular tumour in men over 60, often diffuse large B-cell and bilateral).',
'**Benign prostatic hyperplasia** grows in the transition and periurethral zones under DHT: hesitancy, poor stream, nocturia, retention, bladder hypertrophy, hydronephrosis. It is not premalignant. **Prostatic adenocarcinoma** arises mostly in the **peripheral zone**, so a rectal examination can feel it and urinary symptoms come late. Risk: age, African ancestry, family history, BRCA2. PSA rises (also in BPH, prostatitis and after instrumentation); Gleason grading of gland architecture predicts behaviour. Spread to the spine (via the vertebral venous plexus) gives **osteoblastic** metastases with a raised ALP. Treatment: surveillance, surgery or radiotherapy for local disease; androgen deprivation for advanced disease. Prostatitis: E. coli in older men; Chlamydia and gonorrhoea in younger men.'],
table:{cols:['Tumour','Who','Marker','Clues'],rows:[
['Seminoma','15–35; the commonest','placental ALP; hCG mildly raised in some (syncytiotrophoblast cells); AFP never','sheets of "fried egg" cells, lymphocytes; very radiosensitive; best prognosis'],
['Embryonal carcinoma','20–30','hCG and AFP may rise (usually mixed)','haemorrhage and necrosis; aggressive, early spread'],
['Yolk sac tumour','boys under 3; part of mixed tumours in adults','AFP','Schiller-Duval bodies (glomerulus-like)'],
['Choriocarcinoma','young men','hCG, very high','early blood-borne spread to lung and brain; gynaecomastia, hyperthyroidism'],
['Teratoma','any age','usually none','mature in prepubertal boys (benign); in adult men treated as malignant'],
['Leydig cell tumour','any age','none','Reinke crystals; androgen or oestrogen excess'],
['Lymphoma','over 60','none','commonest testicular tumour in older men']]},
s:[['micro','Epididymitis: under 35, Chlamydia and gonorrhoea (ceftriaxone plus doxycycline); over 35 or after instrumentation, E. coli and other gut organisms (a fluoroquinolone). Pain often eases when the testis is lifted, unlike torsion, though this sign is unreliable. Mumps orchitis follows the parotitis by days and can leave infertility if both testes are hit.']]},
{h:'The ovary',p:[
'Ovarian tumours come from three sources: the surface (Müllerian-type) epithelium, which gives most tumours and most cancers, mainly after 50; germ cells, in girls and young women; and the sex cord-stroma, whose tumours make hormones. Many high-grade serous carcinomas actually begin in the fimbria of the fallopian tube as serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma and then seed the ovary and peritoneum.',
'Epithelial cancer risk rises with the number of ovulations (nulliparity, early menarche, late menopause), **BRCA1/2** and Lynch syndrome; the combined pill, pregnancy, breastfeeding and tubal ligation protect. It is usually found late, with bloating, early satiety and ascites, because spread is across the peritoneum. **CA-125** is used to monitor, not to screen. The ovarian vessels run in the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament, which twists in **ovarian torsion** (sudden pain, often around a cyst or dermoid) and is ligated at oophorectomy; lymph drains to the para-aortic nodes.'],
table:{cols:['Tumour','Clues'],rows:[
['Serous cystadenoma','commonest benign ovarian tumour; thin-walled, often bilateral'],
['Serous carcinoma','commonest malignant ovarian tumour; psammoma bodies; BRCA1/2, TP53; CA-125'],
['Mucinous tumours','large, multilocular; pseudomyxoma peritonei usually means a primary in the appendix'],
['Endometrioid carcinoma','with endometriosis and with endometrial carcinoma'],
['Brenner tumour','usually benign; nests of transitional-type epithelium with "coffee-bean" nuclei'],
['Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid)','commonest ovarian tumour in young women; hair, teeth, sebum; struma ovarii (thyroid tissue, can cause hyperthyroidism); torsion'],
['Immature teratoma','young women; immature neural tissue; malignant'],
['Dysgerminoma','female counterpart of seminoma; LDH, sometimes hCG; radiosensitive'],
['Yolk sac tumour','AFP, Schiller-Duval bodies; girls and young women; aggressive'],
['Granulosa cell tumour','oestrogen: precocious puberty, postmenopausal bleeding, endometrial hyperplasia; Call-Exner bodies; inhibin'],
['Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour','androgens: virilisation, hirsutism'],
['Fibroma','Meigs syndrome: ovarian fibroma, ascites and pleural effusion, which resolve after removal'],
['Krukenberg tumour','bilateral metastases of signet-ring cells, usually from the stomach']]},
s:[['pharm','Epithelial ovarian cancer: debulking surgery, then a platinum drug (carboplatin) and a taxane (paclitaxel). PARP inhibitors (olaparib) are used for BRCA-mutated or repair-deficient tumours: blocking a second DNA repair route kills cells that have already lost homologous recombination. Women with BRCA1/2 may choose risk-reducing removal of tubes and ovaries.']]},
{h:'The cervix, the uterus and the lower tract',p:[
'**Cervical neoplasia**: high-risk HPV (types 16 and 18 most) infects the **transformation zone**, where columnar epithelium becomes squamous. Viral E6 inactivates p53 and E7 inactivates Rb. Dysplasia is graded CIN 1 (lower third, koilocytes, often regresses), CIN 2 and CIN 3 (full thickness, carcinoma in situ); invasive carcinoma is mostly squamous, with adenocarcinoma second. Smoking and immunosuppression (HIV) add risk. Screen with cytology and high-risk HPV testing; the HPV vaccine prevents most cases. Advanced tumours spread sideways and block the ureters, and renal failure is a leading cause of death.',
'**Endometrial hyperplasia** follows unopposed oestrogen; hyperplasia with atypia is the real precursor of cancer. **Endometrial carcinoma** is the commonest gynaecological cancer in high-income countries: type I endometrioid (younger, unopposed oestrogen: obesity, PCOS, anovulation, tamoxifen, nulliparity, late menopause, Lynch syndrome; PTEN and mismatch repair defects; good prognosis) and type II serous (older, atrophic endometrium, TP53, aggressive). Postmenopausal bleeding always needs investigation. **Leiomyosarcoma**: older women, single, soft and haemorrhagic, mitoses and necrosis; it does not arise from a fibroid. **Endometritis** after delivery (especially caesarean) or retained products: fever, tender uterus; clindamycin plus gentamicin.',
'**Vulva**: lichen sclerosus (thin, white, parchment-like skin, usually postmenopausal; a small risk of squamous carcinoma) versus lichen simplex chronicus (thick leathery skin from scratching; no added risk). Vulvar squamous carcinoma is HPV-related in younger women and lichen sclerosus–related in older women. Bartholin cyst or abscess: a tender swelling at the lower vestibule. **Vagina**: clear cell adenocarcinoma in women exposed to diethylstilboestrol in utero; **sarcoma botryoides** (embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, desmin positive) is a grape-like mass in girls under 5; most vaginal squamous carcinoma is extension from the cervix.'],
key:'E6 silences p53; E7 silences Rb. Unopposed oestrogen makes the endometrioid type; age and p53 make the serous type.',
s:[['micro','HPV is a non-enveloped double-stranded DNA virus. Low-risk types 6 and 11 cause genital warts (condylomata acuminata) and laryngeal papillomas; high-risk 16 and 18 cause most cervical, anal and oropharyngeal cancers. The vaccine is made of virus-like particles of the L1 capsid protein and works best before sexual debut.'],['path','Surgical anatomy: beside the cervix the ureter passes beneath the uterine artery, so it is at risk when that artery is clamped at hysterectomy. Uterine prolapse follows weakening of the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 28-year-old has a painless testicular mass and a markedly raised AFP. Which component is present?',o:['Pure seminoma','Choriocarcinoma','Leydig cell tumour','Yolk sac tumour'],a:3,d:'path',c:'testis',x:'AFP comes from yolk sac elements, so a markedly raised AFP means a non-seminomatous component even if most of the tumour looks like seminoma. Choriocarcinoma raises hCG, not AFP.'},
{q:'Which cell makes inhibin B and anti-Müllerian hormone?',o:['Leydig','Sertoli','Spermatogonia','Theca'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'testis',x:'Sertoli cells, driven by FSH, make inhibin B (which feeds back on FSH), androgen-binding protein and anti-Müllerian hormone. Leydig cells make testosterone under LH.'},
{q:'Sildenafil must not be combined with which drug?',o:['Tamsulosin','Finasteride','Nitrates','Metformin'],a:2,d:'pharm',x:'Nitrates raise cGMP by releasing NO and sildenafil stops cGMP being broken down, so together they cause profound hypotension. Tamsulosin can add postural hypotension, but the combination is used with care rather than forbidden.'},
{q:'Ascites, a pleural effusion and an ovarian mass, all resolving after the mass is removed. Tumour?',o:['Granulosa cell tumour','Dysgerminoma','Fibroma','Serous carcinoma'],a:2,d:'path',c:'ovarian-tumours',x:'Meigs syndrome is an ovarian fibroma with ascites and a pleural effusion that disappear after removal. Serous carcinoma also causes ascites, but through peritoneal spread, and removing one ovary does not cure it.'},
{q:'Which zone of the prostate gives rise to most carcinomas?',o:['Peripheral','Transition','Central','Periurethral'],a:0,d:'path',c:'prostate',x:'Most adenocarcinomas arise in the peripheral zone at the back of the gland, which is why a rectal examination can feel them and why urinary symptoms come late. BPH arises in the transition and periurethral zones and obstructs early.'},
{q:'A man with prostate cancer spread to the spine starts continuous leuprolide, and his bone pain worsens in the first week. Why?',o:['Androgen receptor agonism','Initial testosterone surge','Rising oestrogen levels','Osteoblast stimulation'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'prostate',x:'A GnRH agonist first stimulates the pituitary, so LH and testosterone rise for 1–2 weeks before receptor down-regulation shuts them off; starting an androgen receptor blocker first prevents the flare. Degarelix, a GnRH antagonist, suppresses at once with no flare.'},
{q:'High-risk HPV drives cervical carcinoma. What does its E7 protein do?',o:['Inactivates Rb','Degrades p53','Activates RAS','Blocks apoptosis via BCL-2'],a:0,d:'micro',c:'cervix-uterus',x:'E7 binds Rb and frees E2F, so the cell passes the G1/S checkpoint unchecked. E6 is the protein that tags p53 for degradation; together they disable both main tumour suppressors.'}
]};

BOOK['c13-breast']={depth:13,title:'The Milk Ducts',
epi:'Branching ducts under the skin, sealed and quiet. Sometimes a lump forms in the dark, and waits.',
c:['breast','sex-hormones','p53-rb','chemo'],
S:[
{h:'Benign changes',p:[
'The breast is a tree of ducts ending in lobules (terminal duct lobular units), each duct lined by luminal cells and wrapped in myoepithelial cells, set in fat and fibrous stroma held up by suspensory (Cooper) ligaments. Most lumps are benign, and the age of the patient is the first clue. Only **atypical hyperplasia** (ductal or lobular) raises cancer risk substantially; simple fibrocystic change does not.',
'**Gynaecomastia** in men is glandular growth from a high oestrogen-to-androgen ratio: physiological in the newborn, at puberty and in old age; Klinefelter syndrome; cirrhosis (oestrogen is not cleared); testicular tumours (hCG or oestrogen); hyperthyroidism; drugs (spironolactone, ketoconazole, cimetidine, digoxin, anabolic steroids, antiandrogens, some antipsychotics through prolactin).'],
table:{cols:['Lesion','Who','Clue','Cancer risk'],rows:[
['Fibroadenoma','under 35','mobile, rubbery, well defined; grows in pregnancy','not premalignant'],
['Fibrocystic change','25–50','lumpy, cyclic tenderness, blue-domed cysts','none unless atypical hyperplasia'],
['Intraductal papilloma','35–55','bloody discharge from one duct under the areola','slight'],
['Phyllodes tumour','about 50','large, fast-growing; leaf-like overgrowth of stroma','can be malignant (stroma)'],
['Fat necrosis','after trauma or surgery','hard, fixed, calcified; foamy macrophages','none, but mimics cancer'],
['Acute mastitis, abscess','breastfeeding','red, hot, tender; S. aureus','none; keep breastfeeding'],
['Duct ectasia','perimenopausal, smokers','thick green-brown discharge, periductal plasma cells','none']]},
s:[['phys','Oestrogen grows the ducts and progesterone the lobules; prolactin drives milk production once oestrogen and progesterone fall at delivery, and oxytocin contracts the myoepithelial cells to eject milk. Suckling raises both and suppresses GnRH (lactational amenorrhoea). Galactorrhoea outside pregnancy points to high prolactin: prolactinoma, dopamine antagonists, hypothyroidism.']]},
{h:'Breast cancer',p:[
'The commonest cancer in women. Risk follows lifetime oestrogen exposure and genes: age, female sex, early menarche and late menopause, nulliparity or a late first pregnancy, obesity after menopause, combined hormone therapy, alcohol, chest radiation (Hodgkin lymphoma treatment), atypical hyperplasia, family history and **BRCA1/2** (BRCA1 also ovarian cancer; BRCA2 also male breast and prostate cancer), Li-Fraumeni (TP53), Cowden (PTEN). Breastfeeding protects. Most tumours lie in the upper outer quadrant, where most tissue is.',
'**Ductal carcinoma in situ** fills ducts without crossing the basement membrane (comedo necrosis, microcalcification on mammogram). **Paget disease of the nipple**: an eczema-like nipple with large clear cells in the epidermis, signalling an underlying carcinoma. **Invasive ductal carcinoma**: the commonest, a firm stellate mass with a desmoplastic stroma. **Invasive lobular carcinoma**: loss of **E-cadherin**, cells invading in single file, often bilateral and multifocal, hard to see on mammogram. **Lobular carcinoma in situ**: E-cadherin loss, no mass, a risk marker for cancer in either breast. **Medullary** carcinoma (sheets of large cells with lymphocytes, well circumscribed, BRCA1), **mucinous** and **tubular** carcinomas (older women, good prognosis). **Inflammatory carcinoma**: tumour in the dermal lymphatics, red swollen breast, peau d\'orange, poor prognosis.',
'Lymph from most of the breast drains to the **axillary nodes**; medial tumours can drain to the internal mammary chain. Sentinel node biopsy finds the first node in the chain. Axillary surgery can injure the long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior: winged scapula) or the thoracodorsal nerve (latissimus dorsi) and leave arm lymphoedema. Tethered suspensory ligaments dimple the skin. Distant spread goes to bone, lung, liver and brain.'],
table:{cols:['Marker','Treatment','Prognosis'],rows:[
['ER/PR positive','endocrine therapy: tamoxifen, or an aromatase inhibitor after menopause','better'],
['HER2 amplified','trastuzumab, pertuzumab with chemotherapy','much better than before trastuzumab'],
['Triple negative','chemotherapy, immunotherapy; PARP inhibitors if BRCA mutated','worse; BRCA1, younger and Black women']]},
key:'Without distant metastasis, axillary node status is the strongest prognostic factor; then tumour size, grade and receptors. Distant spread outranks them all.'},
{h:'Drugs against the ducts',p:[
'Treatment follows the receptors. A hormone-sensitive tumour needs oestrogen, so block the receptor (tamoxifen), stop oestrogen being made (aromatase inhibitors after menopause, or with ovarian suppression before it), or destroy the receptor (fulvestrant). Aromatase inhibitors fail alone before menopause because the falling oestrogen drives FSH, and the ovaries simply make more. HER2-amplified tumours are treated with antibodies against the receptor or with kinase inhibitors (lapatinib). Triple-negative tumours rely on chemotherapy (anthracyclines, taxanes, platinum), checkpoint immunotherapy and, if BRCA-mutated, PARP inhibitors.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Adverse effects'],rows:[
['Tamoxifen','SERM: blocks ER in breast; agonist in endometrium and bone','endometrial hyperplasia and cancer, VTE, hot flushes; activated by CYP2D6'],
['Raloxifene','SERM: antagonist in breast and uterus, agonist in bone','VTE, hot flushes; no endometrial cancer risk; prevention and osteoporosis'],
['Anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane','aromatase inhibitors: block conversion of androgens to oestrogen','bone loss, arthralgia; postmenopausal women only'],
['Fulvestrant','pure ER antagonist that degrades the receptor','injection-site pain, hot flushes'],
['Trastuzumab, pertuzumab','monoclonal antibodies against HER2','cardiotoxicity, usually reversible (monitor ejection fraction); worse with anthracyclines'],
['Palbociclib, ribociclib','CDK4/6 inhibitors: hold cells in G1','neutropenia; ribociclib prolongs QT'],
['Olaparib','PARP inhibitor: lethal to BRCA-deficient cells','anaemia, nausea; rarely myelodysplasia']]},
s:[['pharm','Anthracyclines (doxorubicin) cause dose-dependent dilated cardiomyopathy through free radicals (dexrazoxane protects); taxanes stabilise microtubules and cause neuropathy. Detail in the Poisoners\' Cure.'],['bio','HER2 (ERBB2) is a receptor tyrosine kinase with no ligand of its own; amplified, it dimerises and drives RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT growth signals. BRCA1 and BRCA2 repair double-strand breaks by homologous recombination, which is why their loss makes PARP inhibition lethal (synthetic lethality).']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 22-year-old has a mobile, rubbery, painless breast lump. Most likely?',o:['Invasive ductal carcinoma','Phyllodes tumour','Intraductal papilloma','Fibroadenoma'],a:3,d:'path',c:'breast',x:'A fibroadenoma is a benign tumour of stroma and glands in young women: mobile, rubbery and well defined. Phyllodes tumours also overgrow the stroma, but occur around 50 and grow fast and large.'},
{q:'Bloody discharge from one nipple duct, with no mass and a normal nipple. Most likely?',o:['Paget disease of the nipple','Lactational mastitis','Intraductal papilloma','Fat necrosis'],a:2,d:'path',c:'breast',x:'A small papillary growth inside a large duct under the areola bleeds into the duct. Paget disease shows as eczema of the nipple skin itself, not as bloody discharge from a normal-looking nipple.'},
{q:'Single-file invading cells that have lost E-cadherin. Tumour?',o:['Invasive ductal','Ductal carcinoma in situ','Medullary carcinoma','Invasive lobular'],a:3,d:'path',c:'breast',x:'E-cadherin holds cells together; without it lobular cells invade singly in lines and are often multifocal and bilateral. Medullary carcinoma is the opposite: a well-circumscribed sheet of large cells with lymphocytes.'},
{q:'A postmenopausal woman has ER-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer. Usual adjuvant hormonal drug?',o:['Trastuzumab','Raloxifene','Letrozole','Fulvestrant'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'breast',x:'After menopause most oestrogen is made by aromatase in fat, so an aromatase inhibitor such as letrozole is the usual adjuvant choice, at the cost of bone loss. Trastuzumab treats HER2-positive disease, and raloxifene is used for prevention, not treatment.'},
{q:'In a woman without distant metastases, which factor best predicts breast cancer prognosis?',o:['Axillary node status','Histological tumour grade','Age at diagnosis','Oestrogen receptor status'],a:0,d:'path',c:'breast',x:'Once distant spread is excluded, the number of involved axillary nodes predicts survival best. Grade and receptor status matter, but less; distant metastasis, when present, outranks every other factor.'},
{q:'After five years of tamoxifen, a woman has postmenopausal bleeding. Why must it be investigated?',o:['Cervical ectropion risk','Endometrial oestrogen agonism','Drug-induced thrombocytopenia','Progesterone receptor block'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'breast',x:'Tamoxifen blocks oestrogen receptors in the breast but stimulates them in the endometrium, raising the risk of hyperplasia, polyps and endometrial carcinoma. Raloxifene lacks this endometrial effect, which is why it is used for prevention.'},
{q:'Which test should be done before and during trastuzumab treatment?',o:['Echocardiogram','Audiogram','Spirometry','Eye check'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'chemo',x:'HER2 signalling helps heart muscle cells survive stress, so blocking it can lower the ejection fraction, usually reversibly; monitor with echocardiography. Audiograms belong to cisplatin and lung function tests to bleomycin.'}
]};
